---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// Reader-facing summary of reports/final/batch-01-synthesis.md. The wording is
// a restatement of that report; no number, ranking or conclusion is added here.
const findings = [
  {
    title: "No dry-mass balance",
    body: "No family has a complete dry-basis balance with compatible whole-butt, filter and input/output denominators. Company accounting and composition splits do not supply one.",
  },
  {
    title: "No analyte-resolved closure",
    body: "No family tracks nicotine, tobacco-specific nitrosamines, representative polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons and cadmium, lead, arsenic and mercury across product, liquids, solids, emissions, purge, treatment outlet and unexplained loss.",
  },
  {
    title: "A named stream is not a sink",
    body: "Naming a wash liquor, a concentrate, an external partner or a recycle loop does not demonstrate where the material finally goes or that treatment works.",
  },
  {
    title: "Endpoints are conflated",
    body: "Identity, recovery, purity, detoxification, leaching and use performance are separate gates. Passing a material characterisation is not passing all of them.",
  },
  {
    title: "No comparable scale or environmental data",
    body: "Energy, reagent, cost and throughput inventories are not comparable across families, so no family can be called cheapest, most expensive or ready to deploy.",
  },
];

const denominators = [
  { claim: "Re-Cig: about 39 % polymer from collected butt mass", reading: "Company accounting on a collected-mass basis; moisture and reject inventory unresolved." },
  { claim: "TchaoMegot: 99.7 % fibre, 0.3 % concentrate", reading: "Company output split; not product purity, analyte removal or a dry-feed balance." },
  { claim: "Keenat: 10–30 % filter in a 70–90 % thermoplastic composite", reading: "Formulation fraction of the output; dilution by matrix is not toxicant removal." },
  { claim: "CN106928486: 58–92 % recovery", reading: "Patent assertion; the input polymer basis and pretreatment normalisation are unresolved." },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What the first research run concluded"
  description="The batch-01 desk synthesis: an evidence boundary rather than a working process, the findings that held, the dissent that was kept, and the one architecture sent forward for review."
  eyebrow="Batch 01"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="page-title">What the first research run concluded</h1>
    <p class="page__lede">
      The best-supported conclusion is an evidence boundary, not a successful recycling process.
      No family is shown to be clean, safe, deployable or environmentally beneficial, and none
      closes dry mass, analyte fate, treatment performance and an independent product gate
      together.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section prose" aria-labelledby="position">
    <h2 id="position">The position in one paragraph</h2>
    <p>
      Six process families were read against the public record and reviewed. Each has a
      source-supported description of what it does, and each has a consequential gap: wash
      loading, recycle purge, treatment performance, sludge composition, solvent closure,
      off-gas capture, pulp yield or product leaching. Where sources disagreed, the disagreement
      is kept rather than averaged. From that record, hypothesis generation produced one
      conditional architecture worth reviewing further, and it is an evidence flow, not an
      operating flowsheet.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      This page summarises the batch-01 desk synthesis, dated 2026-11-07, which covers batch-01
      only: process families F0–F5 and the accepted stage-3 and stage-4 reviews. It is not a
      worldwide inventory and not an exhaustive prior-art search.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="findings">
    <div class="section__heading">
      <h2 id="findings">What held across every family</h2>
      <p class="section__note">Accepted batch-level findings</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      {findings.map((finding) => (
        <>
          <h3>{finding.title}</h3>
          <p>{finding.body}</p>
        </>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose" aria-labelledby="dissent">
    <h2 id="dissent">Where the disagreement was kept</h2>
    <p>
      The review did not resolve these into a winner. They are recorded because a later reader
      would otherwise have to rediscover them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Polymer quality against testability.</strong> The F1 washing architecture was
        chosen because it makes the sink architecture testable, not because it gives the best
        polymer. Where recovered cellulose-acetate quality is the controlling objective, the F3
        dissolution route remains the minority alternative, subject to co-precipitation, metals,
        solvent closure and independent product evidence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wrong stream.</strong> An initial-wash purge may miss the controlling burden if
        that burden sits in the tobacco and ash, and it may create a larger dilute liquid that
        nothing accepts. Only an analyte-specific mass ranking, not a concentration reading,
        separates those cases.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Metals.</strong> No washing, phase-transfer or conversion proposal demonstrates
        metals compliance. Thermal treatment is not elemental destruction, and a blanket
        volatility claim cannot stand in for species-specific fate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dilution and off-gas.</strong> A lower concentration in a composite can come from
        matrix dilution while the less-volatile contaminants stay and the volatile ones leave in
        unmeasured air. Selecting a different architecture does not remove that warning.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Scale.</strong> No family can be ranked on cost or environmental burden from this
        record, because the inventories are incompatible in function, unit and throughput basis.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose" aria-labelledby="proposal">
    <h2 id="proposal">The one architecture sent forward</h2>
    <p>
      The strongest individual proposal was the staged-separation idea built on F1, and the
      primary revision target was a judge-authored combination of it with measured purge,
      separate reject and sludge boundaries, treatment acceptance and outlet evidence, and an
      independent product gate. It is recorded as a new, unvalidated hypothesis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its content is a sequence of questions rather than equipment: characterise the feed and
      separated fractions, rank each analyte's mass by stream, find which reject, liquid or
      product burden actually controls, then test whether a measured purge and a residual
      treatment boundary can hold, and separately test whether the intended product use passes.
      If the controlling burden turns out to be in the tobacco and ash, a fixed initial-wash
      bleed is the wrong answer. If it stays in the product, no named purge rescues it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="action action--quiet" href="/invention/">Read the AI proposal and its falsifiers</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="denominators">
    <div class="section__heading">
      <h2 id="denominators">The numbers that circulate, and what they mean</h2>
      <p class="section__note">Company accounting, not process performance</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Company figures and their denominators">
      <table class="table table--routes">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Four frequently quoted company figures, the denominator behind each, and the reading
          that is permissible.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Figure as published</th>
            <th scope="col">What it actually is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {denominators.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.claim}</th>
              <td>{row.reading}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      None of these is a recovery efficiency, a purity figure or a removal efficiency. They are
      retained with their sources so a later reader can see the basis instead of inheriting a
      number that has lost it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose" aria-labelledby="not-established">
    <h2 id="not-established">What is not established</h2>
    <p>
      No experiment or pilot was run for this synthesis. The accepted record contains no
      comparable cost or energy dataset, no verified permit for any proposed route, no passed
      product-use gate and no demonstrated detoxification. Nothing here says that no company
      holds such evidence privately; the conclusion is limited to what was located, read and
      accepted in this batch.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p class="callout__label">Stop-condition — metals</p>
      <p>
        Cadmium, lead, arsenic and mercury remain the hardest class across every proposal. A
        stream whose metal content is not characterised stays on hold, and no route continues past
        a measured exceedance.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      Desk synthesis only. No experimental validation, no safety claim, no novelty claim and no
      experiment authorised. No patentability, freedom-to-operate, legal, deployment or
      environmental-benefit determination is made.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="page-actions" aria-label="Where to go next">
    <a class="action action--primary" href="/compare/">Compare the assessed routes</a>
    <a class="action action--quiet" href="/limitations/">What this site cannot tell you</a>
  </nav>
</BaseLayout>
